{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CeyCpkaZQSSruhxgnQRs3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I attempted to read the planner/contract/intent source, the existing tests, and the prior review artifacts, but the host returned no file contents for those requests. I therefore ground all code-level claims below in the provided artifact `challenge/005-problem-analysis-v3.md` (the grounded independent problem analysis) and in the provided contract/registry/schema/decision evidence, and I mark anything that rests only on that secondary reading as unverified against source.\n\n# Independent Plan Review\n\n## Approach challenges\n\n**A1 — The whole plan is wrong if `planner.py` is not the production path.**\nThe plan (`planning/001-plan.md`, \"Bounded objective\" and \"Do not alter the legacy agent-tool repair loop\") assumes the deterministic pipeline in `harness/planner.py` is what ships and that the PydanticAI `agent.iter()` tool loop is \"legacy.\" Repository evidence points the other way: `docs/decisions.md` ADR‑014/ADR‑015 describe `ask()` driving `agent.iter()` with `fetch_evidence` repair (`retries=MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS`) as the live control path, and ADR‑018/ADR‑019 wire the shipping `claude_account` provider through that Pydantic‑AI loop. The problem analysis records this as unresolved (Q7, \"Which execution path is production … is unconfirmed\"). *Consequence:* if the agent loop ships, then fixing `planner.py` satisfies the tests but leaves request requirement (5) unmet on the code customers actually hit. *Disproved if:* `run.py`/`harness.agent` wiring shows `plan_and_execute()` is the default dispatch for the default provider, or the agent loop is dead/unreachable code.\n\n**A2 — Collapsing every `bad_request` into `TerminalError` is wrong if any `bad_request` origin is legitimately user-facing.** The plan (\"Change the `bad_request` branch to raise `TerminalError`\") and the contract (`failure_behaviour`: \"The emitter returns bad_request after deterministic planning → raise TerminalError\") treat *all* `bad_request` as operational failure. But `POC_CONTRACT_V0.3_SPEC.md` (\"Error boundary\") classifies a directory‑absent `acara_sml_id` as `bad_request` because \"the harness violated the selector obligation\" — repairable, not internal. A user who types an explicit numeric ID absent from the directory would, under the real emitter, produce `bad_request`, which the plan now turns into an operational crash instead of a clarify/refuse. The problem analysis (Q2, \"three `bad_request` dispositions must be separated\") flags exactly this. *Consequence:* user-caused input errors become `TerminalError` operational stops. *Disproved if:* the planner separates planner-shape defect (failure) from directory-absent explicit ID (user-facing) from release-derived non-match (empty/partial), each with its own test.\n\n**A3 — \"Deterministic derivation\" is only as deterministic as the intent model.** The plan distrusts `StructuredIntent.missing_material_inputs` but still builds requests from `intent.filters` and `intent.requested_metrics` (plan: \"derive all other missing material fields from the assembled filters\"; \"Normalize metric labels … preserving already-canonical values\"). Those slots are themselves LLM-produced (per 005, \"Stage 1 supplies the slots the planner trusts\"). *Consequence:* requirement (1)/(3) determinism is bounded by intent extraction; a metric the model drops or a filter it invents cannot be recovered/rejected by \"derivation.\" *Disproved if:* the planner re-derives selectors from the raw user text rather than from model-populated `StructuredIntent` fields, or `intent.py` is shown to be rule-based rather than model-driven.\n\n## Requirement coverage gaps\n\n**Requirement (4) \"construct exact evidence requests\" is a test assertion, not a runtime guarantee.** The plan constructs \"a fresh request containing explicit intent filters plus only the planner-owned selector,\" but performs no per-question schema validation (`additionalProperties: false`) before dispatch, and explicitly scopes generic validation out (`out_of_scope`: \"Generic local JSON Schema validation for every registered question…\"). Per 005 (Q5) an intent-model-injected unknown filter therefore still reaches the emitter — and under A2 now becomes a `TerminalError`. *Consequence:* \"exact request\" holds only for the three questions with asserted test dictionaries (VC‑POC‑01/03/04); everything else depends on the model. *Disproved if:* the planner validates constructed requests against the pinned per-question schemas and rejects unknown properties before fetch.\n\n**The plan's justification for limiting exact-schema coverage to three questions is contradicted by repository evidence.** The plan's risk note asserts \"the captured pack provides request schemas only for VC‑POC‑01, VC‑POC‑03, and VC‑POC‑04,\" and the contract inherits this (`contract_requirements.exact-schema-coverage`; `out_of_scope`). But `contract/PINNED.json` and the file inventory show hash-pinned request schemas for *every* question (VC‑POC‑01…14, REF‑01/02), and `_index.json` enumerates them all. *Consequence:* the scope restriction may be under-scoping requirement (4) on a false premise; all schemas are present and pinned (ADR‑008) and could back validation. *Disproved if:* `manifest.yaml` shows the Definition Pack deliberately captured only the three schemas AND the method forbids validating against unpinned-in-pack repo files (I could not read `manifest.yaml`; the host returned no content). Distinguish: this is *missing evidence* about the pack boundary, not a demonstrated defect.\n\n**Requirement (2) \"resolve schools safely\" — batching is left ambiguous.** `prompts/rules.md` step 1 requires passing multiple named schools in *one* `search_schools` call. The plan says \"Resolve all named schools as one logical preflight operation,\" which does not commit to a single call versus a loop treated logically as one; whether `EvidenceClient.school_directory` even accepts multiple terms is an unverified gap (005, \"Unresolved evidence gaps: `harness/evidence.py`\"). *Consequence:* possible divergence from `rules.md`. *Disproved if:* `evidence.py` exposes a multi-term directory call and the plan/tests exercise it.\n\n## Unsupported architecture assumptions\n\n**U1 — `plan_and_execute()` is production (see A1).** No provided artifact establishes it; ADR‑014/015/018/019 describe the agent loop as live. *Disproved if:* entrypoint wiring shows otherwise.\n\n**U2 — A runtime input-catalog call is available and safe inside the planner.** The plan states \"Load/cache the input catalog through the existing client/dependencies path when requested metric normalization is needed, and record the preflight in `deps.tool_calls`.\" Yet the vocabularies needed for normalization are *static-closed* and already available offline in `prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json` and the pinned registry `metric_lists`. *Consequence:* introducing a runtime emitter call for stable data adds a failure surface and a non-deterministic dependency to a step the request wants deterministic; it also risks tension with `must_not_change` (\"evidence client protocol\"/\"deterministic data emitter\") if the call path is altered. *Disproved if:* `evidence.py`/`Deps` already expose a catalog accessor the planner can reuse without new I/O, or normalization is shown to require release-derived values (it does not, per OD‑03).\n\n**U3 — Deps supports staged, transactional identity commit.** The plan and `transactional-school-resolution` assume IDs can be accumulated locally and \"committed to Deps only after all mentions resolve.\" 005 notes IDs are \"accumulated directly in shared `Deps` state\"; whether `Deps` cleanly supports a local-then-commit pattern is unverified. *Disproved if:* `harness/tools.py` `Deps` structure permits local staging without side effects.\n\n**U4 — Changing planner-side conditional materiality won't disturb the O‑8 `xfail`.** `docs/decisions.md` O‑8 pins a strict `xfail` in `tests/unit/test_agent_loop.py` asserting the *gate* treats `distance_km` as unconditional, and `contract.material_inputs_for()` (per 005) still widens all `conditional_material` keys. The plan keeps this widening (it lists only 3 files, excluding `contract.py`) and evaluates the condition in the planner. *Consequence:* if any implementation touches `contract.material_inputs_for()`, the strict `xfail` flips and the mandated full-suite-green (`deterministic.full-test-suite`) fails. *Disproved if:* the conditional evaluation is confined to `planner.py` and `test_agent_loop.py`'s `xfail` is left intact.\n\n## Contract testability and achievability\n\n**C1 — `-k` substring gates can pass vacuously.** The `deterministic` checks select by name substring (e.g., `-k \"school or identity or duplicate or conflict\"`, `-k \"metric or ratio or exact_request or normalization\"`). If implementers name tests differently, `-k` deselects everything and pytest exits 0 with no assertions run — a false pass — because the tests don't yet exist and the checks name no minimum count. *Consequence:* `school-resolution-checks`, `input-policy-checks`, `metric-and-request-checks`, `error-semantics-checks` are non-deterministic proofs. *Disproved if:* each check adds a collected-count assertion or enumerates exact test node IDs.\n\n**C2 — `bad-request-failure` over-scopes requirement (5) and conflicts with the v0.3 spec.** The request says \"surface *planner-generated* bad requests as failures.\" The contract's `bad-request-failure`/`failure_behaviour` cover *any* `bad_request` \"reached through deterministic planning,\" which (per A2) includes user-caused directory-absent IDs the spec calls repairable. The contract cannot mechanically distinguish planner-generated from user-generated `bad_request` because the origins are collapsed (005, Q2). *Consequence:* a user error is scored as a required operational crash. *Disproved if:* the contract adds a criterion distinguishing the origins and only requires `TerminalError` for the planner-shape origin.\n\n**C3 — The direct-ID conflict makes `bad-request-failure` achievable only against the stub.** `contract_requirements.direct-id-authority-conflict` preserves explicit ID `99999` yielding empty success (golden case `answer/VC-POC-01-empty`), while `POC_CONTRACT_V0.3_SPEC.md` classifies a directory-absent ID as `bad_request`. This holds only because the *stub* serves empty success (ADR‑012, O‑6 stub/real divergence). Under the real emitter, `99999` → `bad_request` → (per `failure_behaviour`) `TerminalError`, contradicting the golden expectation the contract also obliges (`compatibility_baselines: golden_cases.json`). *Consequence:* the contract is internally satisfiable offline but self-contradictory against the real emitter. *Disproved if:* the Definition Pack resolves the authority conflict (the contract itself defers it), or the golden case is re-pointed.\n\n**C4 — `stable-clarification` \"catalog-backed allowed values where available\" is under-specified.** \"where available\" does not fix which values, in what representation, are asserted; only the *ordering* (\"pinned policy order\") is deterministic. *Disproved if:* the criterion names the exact allowed-value source and shape per field.\n\n**C5 — Achievability depends on unverified stub directory uniqueness.** `safe-school-resolution` requires \"exactly one directory record\" or else clarify; `deterministic.full-test-suite` requires all golden `should_answer` cases (e.g. `answer/VC-POC-01`, `injection/school-name`) still pass. If the stub school directory returns more than one FIXTURE record for \"Alpha College,\" the new ambiguity-clarify flips those cases to clarification and the suite fails. This is *missing evidence* (fixture contents not provided), not a demonstrated defect. *Disproved if:* the stub directory fixture returns exactly one record per golden school name.\n\n**Well-specified criteria (achievable as written):** `no-model-missing-input-trust`, `conditional-materiality`, `exact-metric-meaning` (`[\"Total Enrolments\",\"teaching staff\"] → [\"total_enrolments\",\"teaching_fte\"]`, matching `rules.md`'s authorized alias), `exact-ratio-routing` (VC‑POC‑03 → `ratios`, no `metrics`), and `exact-comparison-request` are concrete and falsifiable; these are the strongest parts of the contract.\n\n## Independent findings disposition\n\nMapping the seven falsifiable questions in `challenge/005-problem-analysis-v3.md`:\n\n- **Q1 (bad_request no longer masked as `Unsupported`; failure signal + negative test) — RESOLVED.** Plan replaces `test_bad_request_returns_unsupported`, raises `TerminalError`, adds a pipeline negative case; contract `bad-request-failure` + `no-user-blame-for-planner-defect`.\n- **Q2 (three `bad_request` origins given distinct dispositions, separately tested) — UNRESOLVED / arguably regressed.** Both plan and contract collapse all origins into one `TerminalError` branch (see A2, C2).\n- **Q3 (ambiguity-clarify + multi-name batching) — PARTIALLY RESOLVED.** Clarify-on-zero/multiple is covered (`safe-school-resolution`); single-call batching is left ambiguous and depends on unverified `evidence.py` (see Requirement gaps).\n- **Q4 (normalize NL metrics to per-question closed vocabulary) — RESOLVED.** Plan adds deterministic case/separator normalization + authorized aliases; contract `metric-normalization`/`metric-routing`/`metric-scope`.\n- **Q5 (planner-side schema validation / `additionalProperties:false` before fetch) — UNRESOLVED.** Explicitly scoped out; unknown filters still reach the emitter (see Requirement gaps, C2).\n- **Q6 (conditional materiality expressed vs widened) — MOSTLY RESOLVED for the single known condition.** Plan evaluates `distance_km` only when `market_definition == distance`; general expression remains widened in `contract.material_inputs_for()` (see U4).\n- **Q7 (which path is production; is (5) enforced on it) — UNRESOLVED and load-bearing.** The plan assumes the planner is production without evidence (see A1/U1).\n\n## Falsifiable questions for reconciliation\n\n1. Does entrypoint wiring (`run.py`/`harness.agent`) dispatch the default provider through `planner.plan_and_execute()`, or through the `agent.iter()` tool loop the plan calls \"legacy\"? (Settles A1/U1/Q7.)\n2. Under the real emitter, does an explicit directory-absent `acara_sml_id` produce `bad_request`, and if so is the contract's mandated `TerminalError` the intended user-facing outcome — or should that origin clarify/refuse? (Settles A2/C2/C3/Q2.)\n3. Does `manifest.yaml` capture request schemas for all questions or only three, and does the method permit validating against the repo's hash-pinned schemas beyond the pack? (Settles the exact-schema scope restriction.)\n4. Is `intent.requested_metrics`/`intent.filters` produced by the LLM intent stage, such that \"deterministic derivation\" still inherits model non-determinism? (Settles A3.)\n5. Does `EvidenceClient.school_directory` accept multiple search terms in one call, as `rules.md` step 1 requires? (Settles the batching gap/Q3.)\n6. Does the stub school-directory fixture return exactly one record per golden school name, so ambiguity-clarify does not regress `should_answer` golden cases under `full-test-suite`? (Settles C5.)\n7. Do the `-k` deterministic checks assert a minimum collected-test count, so a naming mismatch cannot pass them vacuously? (Settles C1.)\n8. Is `contract.material_inputs_for()` left unchanged so the O‑8 strict `xfail` in `test_agent_loop.py` remains failing-as-expected and the full suite stays green? (Settles U4.)\n\n*Boundary note: this is Critic analysis only. It makes no determination of pass/fail, materiality, blocking status, or whether reconciliation is required; those remain Work Controller decisions.*\n\nI should record that source files `harness/planner.py`, `harness/con