{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CeyD6eNhQ8zHhi4SD6TtD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Independent Plan Review\n\nThis plan-review pass reads `planning/001-plan.md` and the current acceptance contract `acceptance/002-contract.yaml` (view `002-contract-review.md`) against the request, the pinned Definition Pack, the returned code (`harness/planner.py`, `contract.py`, `intent.py`, `tools.py`, `evidence.py`, `tests/unit/test_planner.py`), and the grounded analysis `challenge/005-problem-analysis-v3.md`. I make no pass/fail, materiality, blocking, or reconciliation-required determination — those are Work Controller decisions.\n\nTwo prior-review assumptions are settled by evidence returned this turn and should not be re-litigated: `harness/evidence.py` confirms `EvidenceClient.input_catalog()` exists, so the plan's \"load the input catalog through the client\" is a supported capability, not an unsupported assumption; and `contract.material_inputs_for()`/`conditional_material_for()` read the pinned v0.3 `input_policy` (no prose parsing), so the `settings.MATERIAL_INPUTS`/G-4 hazards are already gone.\n\n## Approach challenges\n\n1. **\"Every planner `bad_request` is a harness defect → `TerminalError`\" is contradicted by the contract the plan itself cites.** `harness/planner.py` today maps `bad_request → Unsupported(out_of_scope)` and the plan replaces this with an unconditional `TerminalError` (`error-boundary-refinement`). But `POC_CONTRACT_V0.3_SPEC.md` classifies an `acara_sml_id` absent from the pinned directory as `bad_request` (a *repairable* selector-obligation breach), and the plan deliberately leaves caller-supplied numeric IDs unvalidated (`out_of_scope`, C1). *Consequence:* a user-typed absent ID reaches the emitter and becomes an operational crash for something the contract calls repairable and caller-caused, not a planner-shape defect. *Disproof:* a code path that separates the three `bad_request` origins — planner-shape defect (fail), directory-absent caller ID (not a crash), and release-derived/open non-match (which OD-03 says must be empty/partial, never `bad_request`) — with a test per origin. This is a demonstrated tension, not missing evidence: the SPEC text and the plan's blanket rule cannot both hold.\n\n2. **An out-of-vocabulary *user* metric is treated identically to a planner defect.** The plan enforces the closed vocabulary and states an un-normalizable selector must \"fail before evidence dispatch and not be reinterpreted as an unsupported user question\" (`failure_behaviour`). Mechanical normalization (case/separator + `teaching staff→teaching_fte`) will not resolve a semantic synonym the user actually spoke (e.g. \"enrolment count\"). *Consequence:* a legitimate user phrasing that the model extracted into `requested_metrics` becomes a `TerminalError`/failure rather than a clarification — the inverse failure mode of the one the request targets (system blamed, or user blamed by crash, for a user-input gap). *Disproof:* the contract names a distinct typed outcome (Clarification/Unsupported) for an un-normalizable *user-originated* selector, separate from a planner-constructed malformed request.\n\n3. **\"Exactly one directory record per mention\" can convert answerable questions into clarifications.** `poc_school_directory.v0.3.0.schema.json` `school_search` is a substring/name search; a common name can legitimately return several records. Current code takes `resolved[0]`; the plan mandates clarify on ≠1 (`safe-school-resolution`). *Consequence:* golden `answer/VC-POC-01` etc. could regress to clarifications if their phrasings return >1 record. *Disproof:* evidence that each golden answer phrasing yields exactly one directory record, **or** that golden answer cases are replayed through the legacy loop rather than the planner. *Missing evidence:* no live/fixture directory sample and no `run.py`/`test_agent_loop.py` in this turn to establish which path golden replay drives (ADR-016 warns offline completeness is silence about the live path).\n\n4. **Runtime `--input-catalog` load undercuts \"deterministic.\"** The plan loads the catalog through the client \"when requested metric normalization is needed.\" But `profile_metrics`/`student_context_metrics`/`ratios` are `static_closed` (`poc_input_catalog…schema.json` `x-poc-static-vs-release-derived`) and already pinned in `prompts/input_catalog.snapshot.json` and the registry. *Consequence:* a per-run subprocess call to the data-project emitter for values that are static introduces a data-project dependency and non-determinism into a \"deterministic Stage 2.\" *Disproof:* the planner reads static vocabularies from `contract`/the pinned snapshot rather than a runtime emitter call.\n\n## Requirement coverage gaps\n\n1. **VC-POC-09/10 \"at least one explicit filter beyond the sector default\" (OD-07 / registry `constraints`) is unaddressed.** Deriving materiality from `input_policy.required` (only `peer_filters`/`segment_filters` present) accepts an only-defaults request. `known-condition-only` restricts local condition handling to `distance_km`. *Consequence:* an only-sector-default request passes the planner, the emitter rejects it `bad_request`, and the plan's rule turns that into `TerminalError` — while golden `clarify/peer_filters` and `clarify/segment_filters` expect clarification. *Disproof:* a test asserting clarification (not crash) for an only-default `peer_filters`/`segment_filters` request.\n\n2. **`intent.filters` keys are not whitelisted against the supplied schemas.** `StructuredIntent.filters` is free-form `dict[str, Any]`; the plan builds the request from `dict(intent.filters)` plus the selector. The request schemas are `additionalProperties: false`. *Consequence:* one stray/hallucinated filter key reaches the emitter → `bad_request` → `TerminalError`. Only three schemas are supplied (C2), but at least those three could be enforced pre-dispatch. *Disproof:* planner-side key whitelisting with a rejection test.\n\n3. **VC-POC-03→`ratios` routing authority is not named.** `metric_list_for(\"VC-POC-03\")` is `null`; `StructuredIntent` has only `requested_metrics` (no `requested_ratios`). Routing the single NL slot into `ratios` for VC-POC-03 is a planner inference. The `x-poc-request-field-to-catalog-list` maps `VC-POC-03.ratios → ratios`, but not \"the model's `requested_metrics` becomes the `ratios` field.\" *Consequence:* a hard-coded map risks violating `pinned-policy-authority`. *Disproof:* the contract cites the exact pinned artifact/field that makes VC-POC-03's selector `ratios` derivable rather than hand-maintained.\n\n4. **The `teaching staff→teaching_fte` alias is co-owned by `prompts/rules.md` (must_not_change) and the planner, with no cross-check.** *Consequence:* reworded rules.md prose silently diverges from the planner's alias table. *Disproof:* a drift test binding the planner alias to the pinned rules.md string, analogous to ADR-006/ADR-008 pinning.\n\n5. **Silent drop of `requested_metrics` for no-selector questions is unstated.** For VC-POC-05 (no metric field) the \"fresh request with only the planner-owned selector\" silently discards any `requested_metrics`. Whether that is intended or should surface is not covered by any observable/failure clause.\n\n## Unsupported architecture assumptions\n\n1. **The plan assumes `plan_and_execute` is the production path; repository evidence provided does not establish it.** `harness/tools.py` retains a full legacy agent-loop with its own `bad_request` repair (`_branch_on_error_kind`, `MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS`), and `Deps.intent`/`answer_view` comments note \"None when using the legacy tool-loop path.\" *Consequence:* if the shipping path is the agent loop, requirement (5) is enforced on the wrong module and the whole effort's user-facing effect is unproven. *Disproof:* `run.py`/`harness/agent.py` wiring showing `plan_and_execute` on the live path. *Missing evidence:* neither file was returned this turn.\n\n2. **The plan assumes the golden baseline exercises the planner.** `evals/cases/golden_cases.json` expectations are load-bearing in the contract (`compatibility_baselines`), but whether replay/`test_agent_loop.py` drives them through the planner or the legacy loop is not shown. *Consequence:* exact-request assertions on planner output may not guarantee the golden suite passes if replay drives the legacy loop. *Missing evidence:* replay/agent-loop wiring not provided.\n\n3. **\"Deterministic derivation from intent\" coexists with `request_scope_problem(deps.user_question, …)`.** `harness/tools.py` shows the scope guard the plan preserves reads the raw `user_question`, not the `StructuredIntent`. *Consequence:* `metric-scope` behaviour depends on raw text, so \"the planner is deterministic from the intent\" (ADR/plan framing) is not literally true — a defensible design, but the contract's `metric-scope` and `exact-metric-meaning` criteria inherit a raw-text dependency the plan does not surface. *Disproof:* not required — this is demonstrated in `tools.py`; it should simply be acknowledged.\n\n4. **\"One logical preflight\" is not a single client call.** `EvidenceClient.school_directory(search: str|None, …)` is per-term; the planner will loop N calls. The plan's transactional requirement (local accumulation, commit-after-all) is achievable, but any contract wording implying a single batched call would misdescribe the interface. The contract's `transactional-school-resolution` correctly describes local accumulation, so this is a wording caution, not a defect.\n\n## Contract testability and achievability\n\n1. **Every deterministic gate is exit-code-only with `-k` filters that can pass vacuously.** `full-test-suite` and the four `-k` gates (`002-contract.yaml deterministic`) are machine-checked on exit code only. A `-k \"school or identity or duplicate or conflict\"` selection that matches no test still exits 0 (deselection is not a collection error). *Consequence:* the targeted gates prove nothing about coverage. *Disproof:* add expected pass counts or named-test assertions to each `-k` gate.\n\n2. **`bad-request-failure` is universally quantified but verifiable only under the stub.** The clause asserts *any* emitter `bad_request` \"never returns Unsupported\" and \"raises TerminalError.\" Under the real emitter, ID 99999 returns empty success and an absent-directory ID returns `bad_request` (challenge 1). *Consequence:* the universal wording over-claims relative to what the stub-based suite can verify, and `direct-id-authority-conflict` quietly carves an exception the headline clause denies. *Disproof:* scope the clause to planner-constructed requests, or state the real-emitter divergence explicitly.\n\n3. **\"Fail closed\"/\"fail before dispatch\" does not name the required typed outcome.** For `identity-conflict` and \"a requested selector cannot be normalized,\" `failure_behaviour` says \"fail closed\" / \"fail before evidence dispatch\" without specifying `TerminalError` vs `ClarificationNeeded` vs `Unsupported`. *Consequence:* the criterion is not objectively checkable — two implementations returning different types both \"conform.\" *Disproof:* the contract names the exact typed return for each failure condition.\n\n4. **\"catalog-backed allowed values where available\" is soft.** `stable-clarification` accepts `allowed_values: []`; current `planner.py` always emits `[]`. *Consequence:* the clause is satisfiable by never populating values, even for enumerable material inputs like `market_definition` (`distance|lga|postcode|sa2`). *Disproof:* require populated `allowed_values` for material inputs backed by a static-closed vocabulary.\n\n5. **The O-8 xfail freeze is an implicit, unstated requirement.** The plan fixes conditional `distance_km` in the planner but not in the agent-loop validator; `must_not_change` keeps the legacy loop unchanged, so the strict `xfail` in `test_agent_loop.py` (per `challenge/002-plan-review.md`, attempt 13, which read that file) must remain xfailing for `full-test-suite` to exit 0. *Consequence:* if the change flips it to xpass, the whole suite fails; if it must stay failing, that is a deliberate two-path divergence the contract never states. *Disproof:* the contract explicitly requires the O-8 strict-xfail to remain. *Missing evidence:* I could not re-verify the xfail's presence from files returned this turn; this rests on the prior review.\n\n6. **`exact-schema-coverage` is asymmetric with the observable behaviour.** Exact-request assertions are mandated only for VC-POC-01/03/04, but `metric-routing` and `selector-shape` make claims about VC-POC-11 and multi-school questions with no mandated exact-request check. Achievable, but coverage does not match the asserted behaviour surface.\n\n## Independent findings disposition\n\nMapping `challenge/005-problem-analysis-v3.md`'s falsifiable questions (Q1–Q7):\n\n- **Q1 (is `bad_request` still `Unsupported(out_of_scope)`; is there a failure test?)** — **Resolved by plan/contract.** Branch becomes `TerminalError`; `test_bad_request_returns_unsupported` is replaced; `no-user-blame-for-planner-defect` added.\n- **Q2 (three `bad_request` origins given distinct dispositions + tests?)** — **Unresolved.** The plan collapses to one `TerminalError`; the directory-absent-ID origin is bounded out (C1) and the release-derived non-match (OD-03 → empty/partial) is not distinctly handled. This is the disposition-collapse hazard 005 flagged; it remains open (challenge 1).\n- **Q3 (clarify on multiple + batch mentions?)** — **Partially resolved.** Clarify-on-≠1 and mention-order accumulation are in `safe-/transactional-school-resolution`; single-call batching is not (client is per-term), which the contract accepts.\n- **Q4 (normalize NL metrics to per-question vocabulary?)** — **Partially resolved.** Normalization + routing are specified; **normalization totality** (that every non-canonical value normalizes or fails deterministically) is asserted but not proven achievable (challenge 2).\n- **Q5 (planner-side schema `additionalProperties:false` validation?)** — **Unresolved.** No pre-dispatch validation; `intent.filters` unwhitelisted (gap 2).\n- **Q6 (conditional materiality expressed or widened?)** — **Resolved at the planner level.** The plan evaluates `distance_km` only when `market_definition == distance` (`conditional-materiality`), even though `contract.material_inputs_for` still widens.\n- **Q7 (which path is production; is requirement 5 enforced there?)** — **Unresolved / missing evidence.** No `run.py`/`agent.py` returned (architecture assumption 1).\n\nThe plan resolves the request's headline requirement (5) at the code-design level and correctly overturns the enshrining test. It leaves the disposition-collapse (Q2), pre-dispatch validation (Q5), and production-path (Q7) questions open.\n\n## Falsifiable questions for reconciliation\n\n1. For a user-supplied numeric ID the emitter rejects as `bad_request`, is the intended outcome an operational `TerminalError` crash, or a graceful outcome — and what mechanically distinguishes a planner-constructed malformed request from a passed-through user/identity error? (*Disproof:* a discriminator plus a test per origin.)\n2. Is metric normalization provably total over the pinned closed vocabularies, and what typed outcome does an un-normalizable *user* selector receive versus a planner-shape defect? (*Disproof:* a normalization-failure test asserting a specific typed return.)\n3. Does the planner enforce VC-POC-09/10 \"at least one filter beyond the sector default,\" returning clarification rather than dispatching into a `bad_request → TerminalError`? (*Disproof:* an only-default clarify test.)\n4. Are `intent.filters` keys whitelisted against the three supplied `additionalProperties:false` schemas before dispatch? (*Disproof:* a stray-key rejection test.)\n5. Which pinned artifact makes VC-POC-03's selector `ratios` (and VC-POC-11's `student_context_metrics`) derivable rather than a hand-maintained map, satisfying `pinned-policy-authority`?\n6. Is `plan_and_execute` on the live path (`run.py`/`harness/agent.py`), and do the cited golden baselines exercise it or only the legacy loop?\n7. What exact typed outcome (`TerminalError` / `ClarificationNeeded` / `Unsupported`) is required for `identity-conflict` and for an un-normalizable selector, so the `failure_behaviour` clauses become objectively checkable?\n8. Do the `-k` deterministic gates require a minimum matched-test count or named tests, so they cannot pass vacuously?\n9. Is the O-8 strict-`xfail` in `test_agent_loop.py` required to remain failing after the planner-side conditional-materiality fix, and is that two-path divergence a stated requirement?\n10. Does the planner read static vocabularies from the pinned snapshot/registry, or call the live emitter `--input-catalog` at runtime — and how is determinism preserved either way?\n\n*Boundary note: this is Critic analysis only. It proposes no code changes and makes no determination of pass/fail, materiality, blocking status, or reconciliation; those remain Work Controller decisions.*"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":134326,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":134326},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86daf58c-a779-4783-8f6b-7d1e686c38f9","uuid":"c5f975d6-0cbd-4595-af19-81bdbd9f7401","request_id":"req_011CeyD6c81qgJVMAWDbB39Y"}
